{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.BranchConvexityBound","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BranchConvexityBound","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.BigOperators.Ring.Finset","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Fin","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Fin.VecNotation","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FinCases"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.larger_index_set_escapes_the_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.larger_index_set_escapes_the_bound","kind":"theorem","line":97,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BranchConvexityBound.lean#L97-L117","doc":"**What escapes: a larger index set.**  The bound is relative to the branch\nset it is taken over.  Enlarging that set can raise the maximum and admit values\nthe old bound excluded — here `2`, which no convex combination over `{0}` could\nreach.\n\nThis is the satellite/cabling move in miniature.  A satellite replaces each\nstrand by several, so its branches are words in a *larger* algebra: not a\nreweighting of the old branches but a different index set, outside the\nhypotheses of `not_reaches_of_bound_lt`.  It is why front 2 closes the\nreweighting route without touching the satellite route. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.bound_fails_without_nonneg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.bound_fails_without_nonneg","kind":"theorem","line":85,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BranchConvexityBound.lean#L85-L95","doc":"**Non-negativity is load-bearing.**  Drop `hw` and the bound fails: weights\n`(2, -1)` still sum to `1`, but `2·1 + (-1)·0 = 2` exceeds `max = 1`.  So\n`convex_comb_le_bound` is not a tautology about sums — it is convexity doing the\nwork, which is exactly what `atom-branch-convexity-bound` had to *measure*\n(`every w_b > 0`) rather than assume. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.convex_comb_satisfiable","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.convex_comb_satisfiable","kind":"theorem","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BranchConvexityBound.lean#L80-L83","doc":"The hypotheses are satisfiable: a point mass is a convex combination. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.not_reaches_of_bound_lt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.not_reaches_of_bound_lt","kind":"theorem","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BranchConvexityBound.lean#L65-L73","doc":"**The exclusion.**  A target strictly above every branch value is\nunreachable by any convex reweighting.  Applied: `M = 1.018275` is the measured\n`max_b tr_M(β_b)` over the 32 deuteron branches and `target = 1.491498` is\n`√B_AME`, so no reweighting — the colour-tube refinement included — gets there. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.convex_comb_le_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.convex_comb_le_bound","kind":"theorem","line":52,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BranchConvexityBound.lean#L52-L63","doc":"**A convex combination is bounded by the largest term.**  With `t b` the\nbranch traces and `w` the branch weights, this is\n`tr_M(atom) = ∑_b w_b tr_M(β_b) ≤ max_b tr_M(β_b)`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}